Breadth-First Search (BFS) is a graph traversal algorithm that explores nodes layer by layer. In BFS, starting from a selected node (or root node), we explore all its adjacent (neighboring) nodes before moving deeper into the graph. This traversal technique uses a queue data structure to keep track of nodes to be visited next. Here’s a step-by-step explanation:

Step-by-Step Process of BFS:

Initialization:
Choose a starting node (the root node for traversal).
Initialize a queue and enqueue the starting node.
Mark this node as visited.

Visit Neighbors:
Dequeue the front node of the queue and examine it.
For each unvisited neighbor of this node, mark it as visited and enqueue it into the queue.

Repeat:
Repeat the dequeue, visit, and enqueue steps until the queue is empty, meaning all nodes connected to the starting node have been visited.

End:
When the queue is empty, BFS traversal is complete.
Example Walkthrough
Imagine a graph with the following structure, with edges between nodes A, B, C, D, E, and F:

mathematica
Copy code
       A
      / \
     B   C
    /|   |
   D E   F
Suppose we start BFS traversal at node A.

Step 1: Start at A. Enqueue A and mark it as visited.

Queue: [A]

Step 2: Dequeue A, visit it, and enqueue its neighbors B and C.

Visited Order: A

Queue: [B, C]

Step 3: Dequeue B, visit it, and enqueue its unvisited neighbors D and E.

Visited Order: A, B

Queue: [C, D, E]

Step 4: Dequeue C, visit it, and enqueue its unvisited neighbor F.

Visited Order: A, B, C

Queue: [D, E, F]

Step 5: Dequeue D and visit it. D has no unvisited neighbors, so move on.

Visited Order: A, B, C, D

Queue: [E, F]

Step 6: Dequeue E and visit it. E has no unvisited neighbors.

Visited Order: A, B, C, D, E

Queue: [F]

Step 7: Dequeue F and visit it. F has no unvisited neighbors.

Visited Order: A, B, C, D, E, F

Queue: [] (empty)

End: The queue is empty, and BFS traversal is complete.

BFS Key Points
Layer-by-layer traversal: BFS explores all nodes at the current depth before moving deeper.
Queue usage: Ensures that nodes are visited in the correct order.
Applications: BFS is useful for finding the shortest path in unweighted graphs, discovering connected components, and level-order traversal in trees.

#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

#define MAX 10 // Maximum number of vertices

// Structure to represent a node in an adjacency list
struct Node {
    int vertex;
    struct Node* next;
};

// Structure to represent a graph
struct Graph {
    int numVertices;
    struct Node* adjLists[MAX];
    int visited[MAX];
};

// Queue structure for BFS
struct Queue {
    int items[MAX];
    int front;
    int rear;
};

// Function prototypes
struct Node* createNode(int);
struct Graph* createGraph(int);
void addEdge(struct Graph*, int, int);
void bfs(struct Graph*, int);
struct Queue* createQueue();
void enqueue(struct Queue*, int);
int dequeue(struct Queue*);
int isEmpty(struct Queue*);

int main() {
    int vertices, edges, startVertex;
    int u, v;

    printf("Enter the number of vertices: ");
    scanf("%d", &vertices);
    
    struct Graph* graph = createGraph(vertices);

    printf("Enter the number of edges: ");
    scanf("%d", &edges);
     int i;
    for ( i = 0; i < edges; i++) {
        printf("Enter the edge (u v): ");
        scanf("%d %d", &u, &v);
        addEdge(graph, u, v);
    }

    printf("Enter the starting vertex for BFS: ");
    scanf("%d", &startVertex);

    printf("BFS Traversal: ");
    bfs(graph, startVertex);

    return 0;
}

// Create a new node
struct Node* createNode(int v) {
    struct Node* newNode = malloc(sizeof(struct Node));
    newNode->vertex = v;
    newNode->next = NULL;
    return newNode;
}

// Create a graph with the specified number of vertices
struct Graph* createGraph(int vertices) {
    struct Graph* graph = malloc(sizeof(struct Graph));
    graph->numVertices = vertices;
    int i;
    for (i = 0; i < vertices; i++) {
        graph->adjLists[i] = NULL;
        graph->visited[i] = 0;
    }

    return graph;
}


// Add an undirected edge to the graph
void addEdge(struct Graph* graph, int u, int v) {
    struct Node* newNode = createNode(v);
    newNode->next = graph->adjLists[u];
    graph->adjLists[u] = newNode;

    newNode = createNode(u);
    newNode->next = graph->adjLists[v];
    graph->adjLists[v] = newNode;
}

// Create a queue
struct Queue* createQueue() {
    struct Queue* queue = malloc(sizeof(struct Queue));
    queue->front = -1;
    queue->rear = -1;
    return queue;
}

// Check if the queue is empty
int isEmpty(struct Queue* queue) {
    return queue->front == -1;
}

// Add an element to the queue
void enqueue(struct Queue* queue, int value) {
    if (queue->rear == MAX - 1) {
        printf("Queue is full\n");
        return;
    } else {
        if (queue->front == -1) queue->front = 0;
        queue->items[++queue->rear] = value;
    }
}

// Remove an element from the queue
int dequeue(struct Queue* queue) {
    int item;
    if (isEmpty(queue)) {
        printf("Queue is empty\n");
        return -1;
    } else {
        item = queue->items[queue->front];
        if (queue->front >= queue->rear) { // Reset the queue after last dequeue
            queue->front = -1;
            queue->rear = -1;
        } else {
            queue->front++;
        }
        return item;
    }
}

// BFS traversal
void bfs(struct Graph* graph, int startVertex) {
    struct Queue* queue = createQueue();

    graph->visited[startVertex] = 1;
    enqueue(queue, startVertex);

    while (!isEmpty(queue)) {
        int currentVertex = dequeue(queue);
        printf("%d ", currentVertex);

        struct Node* temp = graph->adjLists[currentVertex];

        while (temp) {
            int adjVertex = temp->vertex;

            if (graph->visited[adjVertex] == 0) {
                graph->visited[adjVertex] = 1;
                enqueue(queue, adjVertex);
            }
            temp = temp->next;
        }
    }
}

Let’s walk through a step-by-step example using the DFS iterative approach on a sample graph.

Example Graph Structure
Consider the following graph:

plaintext
Copy code
     A
    / \
   B   C
  / \   \
 D   E   F
      \
       G
We can represent this graph as an adjacency list (dictionary) in Python:

python
Copy code
graph = {'A': ['B', 'C'],'B': ['D', 'E'], 'C': ['F'], 'D': [], 'E': ['G'], 'F': [], 'G': []}

Step-by-Step DFS Iterative Walkthrough
Let’s perform DFS starting from node 'A' and trace each step.

Initial Setup
Stack: ['A'] (Start with the root node)
Visited Set: {} (Empty initially)
Step-by-Step Execution

Pop 'A' from Stack:
Stack: []
Visited Set: {'A'}
Process 'A': Print 'A'.
Push Neighbors of 'A' (C, B) to Stack: Add 'C' and 'B' in reverse order so that 'B' is on top.

After this step:
Stack: ['C', 'B']

Pop 'B' from Stack:
Stack: ['C']
Visited Set: {'A', 'B'}
Process 'B': Print 'B'.
Push Neighbors of 'B' (E, D) to Stack: Add 'E' and 'D' in reverse order so that 'D' is on top.

After this step:
Stack: ['C', 'E', 'D']

Pop 'D' from Stack:
Stack: ['C', 'E']
Visited Set: {'A', 'B', 'D'}
Process 'D': Print 'D'.
D has no neighbors, so we don't add anything to the stack.

After this step:
Stack: ['C', 'E']

Pop 'E' from Stack:
Stack: ['C']
Visited Set: {'A', 'B', 'D', 'E'}
Process 'E': Print 'E'.
Push Neighbor of 'E' (G) to Stack.

After this step:
Stack: ['C', 'G']

Pop 'G' from Stack:
Stack: ['C']
Visited Set: {'A', 'B', 'D', 'E', 'G'}
Process 'G': Print 'G'.
G has no neighbors, so we don't add anything to the stack.

After this step:
Stack: ['C']

Pop 'C' from Stack:
Stack: []
Visited Set: {'A', 'B', 'D', 'E', 'G', 'C'}
Process 'C': Print 'C'.
Push Neighbor of 'C' (F) to Stack.

After this step:
Stack: ['F']

Pop 'F' from Stack:
Stack: []
Visited Set: {'A', 'B', 'D', 'E', 'G', 'C', 'F'}
Process 'F': Print 'F'.
F has no neighbors, so the stack remains empty.

Final State
Stack: [] (empty, so we stop)
Visited Order: A B D E G C F

Output
The nodes were processe'd in this DFS order: A, B, D, E, G, C, F.